<p>
I follow Denise and Gordon back down to Idaho Springs in the last of the light, where we meet up with her husband Bud and their two kids, just back from skiing at Loveland.  [Three of them skiing, your note says; who?]
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.1/DSCN0268.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.1/Small/DSCN0268.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.1/DSCN0271.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.1/Small/DSCN0271.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.1/DSCN0272.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.1/Small/DSCN0272.jpg"></a>
<p>
Pizza, and they treated me, so I paid the tip in cash.  [Which pizza place?]  Then I go across the street to the coffee house to escape the cold and get online.  Coffee refills are 25 cents, what a deal.  Internet is $3 an hour or $9 for all day.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.1/DSCN0273.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.1/Small/DSCN0273.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.1/DSCN0275.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.1/Small/DSCN0275.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.1/DSCN0276.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.1/Small/DSCN0276.jpg"></a>
<p>
Here's what I wrote from the coffee house that night: I'm now finding myself in Idaho Springs, sitting in a coffee shop and hiding out from the cold.  Shortly the barista will be kicking me out, for the clock will soon roll around to 7:30, signaling the end of the business day.  So down the street I'll meander, to any ole bar, to extend my current addiction for anything and everything warm.  You see, the nights are cold up here in the mountains, and tonight's forecast calls for a low of something ridiculous like 11 degrees.  I've never slept in 11-degree weather.  While ill-prepared psychologically for my upcoming slumber, I have at least prepared for it in a practical sense: the two Indian blankets, five layers of covers, a sleeping bag with a hoodie, winter underwear and half a brain.
<p>
In all honesty, my primary concern about the cold isn't for my own well-being.  No.  It's for the well-being of my laptop.  Running a laptop in very cold temperatures is never a good idea, the hard drive just doesn't like it.  So how do I keep the laptop warm?  Since the primary source of heat at night (the only source, for that matter) is me, I will have to sleep with my laptop.  Yeah, sounds a little strange, maybe even kinky.  But if I roach my laptop, my travel writing is pretty much screwed.  Hmmmm, suppose I were to fall asleep and never wake again because I froze to death.  What would the eulogy be like?  "He loved life, he loved his friends, he loved his family, and he loved his laptop computer, right up until the very end.  Loved the laptop computer, that is..."
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.2/DSCN0001.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.2/Small/DSCN0001.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.2/DSCN0004.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.2/Small/DSCN0004.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.2/DSCN0007.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.2/Small/DSCN0007.jpg"></a>
<p>
Kicked out at 7:30 as promised, I walk down the street looking for a mailbox for my Graceland postcards, freeze, and retreat into Tommyknockers to warm up.  They brew their own beer.  Mmmmm, beer.  The stout is pretty good.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.2/DSCN0013.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.2/Small/DSCN0013.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.2/DSCN0015.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.2/Small/DSCN0015.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.2/DSCN0017.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.2/Small/DSCN0017.jpg"></a>
<p>
The brewery tanks, the sign, and the thermometer outside the door: 9 degrees.  It's 10:30 and I'm tired, but I don't want to leave the bar.  I'm like a drunk who just wants one more, only my addiction tonight is heat, not alcohol.  Sometimes it's tempting to hook up with someone from the bar, if only to keep warm.  Maybe I need to get a dog from the pound, use it for two weeks, then return it.  Geeze, that sounds pretty bad.  Thoughts of being cold put strange thoughts in a man's head.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.2/DSCN0019.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.2/Small/DSCN0019.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.2/DSCN0028.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.2/Small/DSCN0028.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.2/DSCN0034.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.2/Small/DSCN0034.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.2/DSCN0039.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.2/Small/DSCN0039.jpg"></a>
<p>
In the end I don't sleep in Idaho Springs at all.  I point the van west on I-70, through the Eisenhower Tunnel in the snow, and the driving log says the day ends in Frisco, 94 miles from Paul's.  [Where did you park for the night, and why Frisco?]  Tomorrow we find out how the five layers did.
<p>
